        <h2>Where this started</h2>
        <p>Bailey Terminals grew out of exchange-grade metals warehousing — work where inventory has to reconcile to the ounce and the paperwork has to survive an outside audit, not just an internal one. That's a higher bar than most general warehousing operations are built to, and it shaped how we run every part of the business since.</p>
        <p>We've since opened that same discipline up to customers who aren't warehousing exchange metal at all — resin producers routing around container shortages, fabricators who just need overflow yard space, shippers who need somewhere for a railcar to sit that isn't costing them per diem.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>How we operate</h2>
        <p>Two rail-served sites, run directly — not subcontracted, not brokered. When you call, you're talking to the people who actually control the yard, not a layer of account management between you and the answer.</p>
        <p>We'd rather tell you plainly what we can and can't do than oversell capacity we don't have. If we're full, we'll say so.</p>
      </div>
